  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nSandwiched between a trough in the Pacific Northwest and a ridge \nover the Southern Plains, southwesterly flow is dominant aloft over \nthe Desert Southwest. This flow is advecting in a rich plume of \ntropical Pacific moisture, with PWATs near an inch across the \nregion. The one caveat to this deep moisture are the lingering dry \nsurface layers. This evening's 00z KGJT sounding shows a pronounced \ninverted-V profile, with the column saturated aloft but quite dry \nnear the surface. As a result, most locations have seen virga \nshowers and strong, gusty winds this evening, rather than heavy \nrainfall. Areas in northeast Utah where showers have been training \nfor the last 6 hours have finally seen precipitation make it to the \nsurface, but totals have been limited to just a few hundredths. With \nplenty of moisture but a lack of instability, this type of activity \nwill continue through the night, with gusty winds and light rainfall \nthrough daybreak tomorrow. \n\nDrier air moves in after daybreak, bringing an end to any lingering \nshowers and allowing skies to clear, setting the stage for a drier \nweek ahead. The Pacific Northwest trough tracks along the Canadian \nborder over the coming days, while high pressure builds westward \ninto the Desert Southwest, pushing a ridge up along the West Coast. \nDrier zonal flow will be in control over eastern Utah and western \nColorado by midweek, allowing temperatures to warm up a few degrees, \nrunning around 5 degrees above normal. Enough surface moisture will \nlinger to fuel a few isolated showers or thunderstorms over the \nhigher terrain Monday and Tuesday, but chances dwindle by\nmidweek.",
